<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>The table shows the masses, in kg (to the nearest kg), of <m>{65}</m> sacks of potatoes delivered to a market in Nyandarua.</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Mass (kg)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m>–<m>{19}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m>–<m>{29}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m>–<m>{39}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m>–<m>{49}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{50}</m>–<m>{59}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{60}</m>–<m>{69}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{4}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{9}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{15}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{18}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{9}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>For grouped data, the median or a quartile at a given position is estimated by <me> L + \frac{\text{position} - CF}{F} \times c </me>where <m>L</m> is the lower class boundary of the class that holds the position, <m>CF</m> the cumulative frequency before that class, <m>F</m> its frequency and <m>c</m> the class width.</p><p>(a) Find the position of the lower quartile, <m>Q_1</m>.</p><div>Position <m>=</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find <m>Q_1</m>, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><div><m>Q_1 =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> kg  </div><p>(c) Find the position of the upper quartile, <m>Q_3</m>.</p><div>Position <m>=</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/>  </div><p>(d) Find <m>Q_3</m>, correct to 1 decimal place.</p><div><m>Q_3 =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans4"/> kg  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p><m>Q_1</m> has a quarter of the data at or below it. For grouped data the positions are <m>n/4</m> and <m>3n/4</m>, with no <m>+1</m>, because a position is a point on a continuous scale. Inside a class we assume the values are spread evenly from the lower class boundary to the upper, so we go the right fraction of the way across the class.</p><p>The masses are to the nearest kg, so the class <m>{10}</m>–<m>{19}</m> starts at the lower class boundary <m>{9.5}</m>, and so on.</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Class</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency (<m>f</m>)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Cumulative frequency (cf)</cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m>–<m>{19}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{4}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{4}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m>–<m>{29}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{9}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{13}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m>–<m>{39}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{15}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{28}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m>–<m>{49}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{18}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{46}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{50}</m>–<m>{59}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{56}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{60}</m>–<m>{69}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{9}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{65}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>(a) <m>n = {65}</m>, so <m>Q_1</m> is at position <m>{65} \div 4 = {16.25}</m>.</p><p>(b) The first cumulative frequency to reach <m>{16.25}</m> is <m>{28}</m>, so <m>Q_1</m> is in the class <m>{30}</m>–<m>{39}</m>: <m>L = {29.5}</m>, <m>CF = {13}</m>, <m>F = {15}</m>, <m>c = 10</m>. <me>Q_1 = {29.5} + \frac{ {16.25} - {13} }{ {15} } \times 10 \approx {31.7}\text{ kg}</me></p><p>(c) <m>Q_3</m> is at position <m>3 \times {65} \div 4 = {48.75}</m>.</p><p>(d) The first cumulative frequency to reach <m>{48.75}</m> is <m>{56}</m>, so <m>Q_3</m> is in the class <m>{50}</m>–<m>{59}</m>: <m>L = {49.5}</m>, <m>CF = {46}</m>, <m>F = {10}</m>. <me>Q_3 = {49.5} + \frac{ {48.75} - {46} }{ {10} } \times 10 \approx {52.3}\text{ kg}</me></p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{65}{4}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{95}{3}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{195}{4}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{209}{4}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>